# {{.project_name}}

An evaluation project for an AI agent: a governed dataset, versioned
scorers, a pinned baseline, a promotion gate, and evidence that lands in
MLflow and Unity Catalog.

## The loop

New to evaluating LLMs? Read the short version first: **an experiment is a
comparison, not a log.** Running your agent once and reading the answers
tells you almost nothing — the answers always look plausible. What tells you
something is scoring this version against the previous one, on the same
data, with the same scorers. That is the loop this project is built around,
and `agentkit compare` is the verb that runs it.

```bash
python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py     # isolated setup, no credentials
make install-ci                     # or `make install` with workspace auth

agentkit smoke                      # seconds, free, no cluster
agentkit compare --establish-baseline
                                    # scores the current version and records
                                    # it AS the baseline

# now change something — edit src/app/example_agent.py
agentkit compare                    # is this better than what we had?
agentkit gate                       # promotion check (exit 0 pass / 2 fail)
agentkit evidence                   # the release record for a reviewer
```

`src/app/example_agent.py` is a real, runnable agent — a policy guard plus
keyword retrieval over a small knowledge base. Edit an entry and rerun
`agentkit compare` to watch the metrics move. When you are ready to evaluate
your own agent, point `agent:` in `agentkit.yaml` at it; nothing else in the
project changes.

Full background: `docs/agent-evaluation.md` in the platform repository.

## Configuration

`agentkit.yaml` is three lines. Everything else is inferred from what the
dataset rows contain, and every optional key in that file is an escape
hatch, not the normal path.

```yaml
version: 1
agent: src/app/example_agent.py:respond
dataset: evals/data/golden_cases.json
```

Before every judged run the tool prints the plan it inferred, the scorer
versions it will use, and what the judge calls will cost — then asks before
spending anything. `agentkit smoke` costs nothing by construction: it runs
only the deterministic code scorers.

## Two speeds

| | Where | When | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `agentkit smoke` | your laptop, PR CI | every commit | Seconds. No cluster, no credentials, no judge spend. |
| `agentkit eval` | Databricks job | pre-merge, pre-promote | The datasets and production traces already live in Unity Catalog; compute goes to the data and the results land in the record with no upload step. |

Smoke deliberately does not create an MLflow run: it needs nothing from
MLflow, which is what keeps it credential-free, and it keeps a morning of
throwaway runs out of your experiment. `compare` and `eval` record.

`agentkit eval --submit` runs the bundle's `release_gate` job. Deploy it
with `databricks bundle validate -t dev` and `databricks bundle deploy -t
dev`; uncomment the cron block in `resources/evaluation_job.yml` for
continuous evaluation.

## Scorers come from the shared registry

```bash
agentkit scorers ls
```

Scorer names, judge models, judge prompts, input contracts, and scales are
versioned platform assets. This project *selects* scorers and sets
thresholds; it never defines what one means. That is what makes a
`correctness/mean` of 0.8 comparable between two teams.

Which scorers run is inferred from the dataset: rows with
`expectations.expected_response` get correctness and the deterministic
coverage scorers; rows carrying retrieval traces get groundedness. Scorers
whose contract the data cannot satisfy are listed as excluded, with the
reason, rather than silently skipped.

Judges are LLMs and need calibrating against human labels before you trust
them in a gate — see `notebooks/01_align_judge.py`. The
`pension_domain_policy` judge is report-only until that calibration exists.

## Datasets

- `evals/data/golden_cases.json` — the reviewed regression suite that gates
  releases. Grow it toward 150+ cases and turn every production failure into
  a case.
- `evals/data/edge_cases.json` — the capability suite: deliberately hard,
  report-only.
- `evals/data/answer_sheet.json` — recorded answers, so scoring works
  without calling the application.
- `scripts/sync_dataset.py` publishes cases to the governed Unity Catalog
  dataset `{{.catalog}}.{{.schema}}.{{.dataset_name}}`.

## The gate and the record

`agentkit gate` refuses an empty answer to "what did you compare against":
a run with no named baseline is an incomplete submission, and a threshold
whose metric never appeared fails closed. Exit codes are the CI contract —
`0` passed, `2` ran but a threshold failed, `1` a configuration or runtime
error.

`agentkit evidence` writes `evidence.md` and `evidence.json`: what ran, on
which dataset version, scored by which scorer and prompt versions, against
which baseline, with which verdict and whose approval. Attach it to the
promotion request.

For a run that happened somewhere else — the deployment-job gate runs on a
job cluster whose filesystem nobody can reach afterwards — use
`agentkit evidence --run <mlflow run id>`. Every recorded run attaches its
results record to itself, and the scoring command prints that command when it
finishes.

`resources/optional/` holds the Unity Catalog registered model and the
deployment-job gate, which adds a human approval step to every new model
version. They are opt-in — read the comments in those files before enabling
them, particularly the note that a deployment job's first run always stops
at the approval task by design.

## CI

Pull requests run `make check`, which includes `evals/offline_checks.py`
(`agentkit smoke`) with zero credentials. The credentialed `release_gate`
job runs after merge. The platform team must onboard this repository
(main-branch federated credential, service principal, constrained compute,
non-secret repository variables) before the deploy workflow is enabled.

Run `make help` for every target.
